course: dictionary
lesson: 6777
title: Words for people and places
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An optician is a person whose job is to test people's eyes and make or sell glasses and contact lenses. Context helps you see what the word means: «Regular eye checks with an optician can catch problems early» tells you the optician checks eyes for problems.
explain: The same word can mean a place too. «The optician's» with an apostrophe s is the shop where the optician works. Ask yourself: is the sentence about a person doing something, or about a place you go to?
explain: «If you are told you need glasses, wear them as the optician says, because they help you see clearly and learn better.» Here the optician is the person who knows about eyes, so their advice matters. Always check the words around the word to find its meaning.
fact: An optician is a person who tests eyes and makes or sells glasses and contact lenses.
fact: The optician's is the shop where an optician works.
fact: You go to the optician's for an eye test.
fact: «Children who cannot see well often do not know it, so eye tests at school or an optician matter.»
fact: Some words name a person who does a job, and their workplace often has the same name with 's added, as in the optician's.
exercise: «Regular eye checks with an optician can catch problems early.» What is an optician? | A person who sells cars | A teacher who runs a school library | *A person whose job is to test eyes and make or sell glasses | A doctor who only works in a hospital
exercise: Mum says, «We are going to the optician's this afternoon for my eye test.» Where are they going? | To the bakery for bread | To the library to borrow books | To the football ground | *To the shop where eyes are tested and glasses are made
exercise: «If you are told you need glasses, wear them as the optician says.» What should you do? | Wear them only at the beach | *Wear the glasses as the optician says | Wear them only when you play football | Throw the glasses away
test: «Zara's optician asked her how long she plays.» Who asked the question? | Her grandad | Her best friend | *The optician | Her teacher
test: «Children who cannot see well often do not know it.» Why do eye tests matter? | *They can find problems early, so children can see and learn well | Only grown-ups need eye tests | Eye tests are only for fun | Eye tests make your eyes change colour
exam: «Go to the optician's for an eye test.» In this sentence, where do you go? | A football pitch | *A shop where eyes are tested and glasses are made | A bakery | A library
